1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,050
Elizabeth Choy, the Canteen
Operator Who Wouldn't Break

2
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:08,185
Cross the junction where Orchard
Road meets Stamford Road,

3
00:00:08,265 --> 00:00:12,691
opposite the National Museum of
Singapore, and there is a YMCA

4
00:00:12,771 --> 00:00:17,350
building with a red logo on its
tower and buses idling outside.

5
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:21,296
Almost nobody waiting there
knows that the building on this

6
00:00:21,376 --> 00:00:25,186
exact spot was once the
Kempeitai's East District Branch,

7
00:00:25,266 --> 00:00:29,826
and that a schoolteacher named
Elizabeth Choy spent roughly 200 days

8
00:00:29,906 --> 00:00:34,000
in a cell in it, tortured for
information she never gave up.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,117
Elizabeth Choy was born
Yong Su-Moi on the 29th of

10
00:00:38,197 --> 00:00:43,150
November 1910 in Kudat, in
British North Borneo, now Sabah.

11
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:47,466
Her father was a civil servant
who rose to District Officer,

12
00:00:47,546 --> 00:00:51,641
and she was raised in her
early years by a Kadazan nanny,

13
00:00:51,721 --> 00:00:54,725
picking up Kadazan as her first language.

14
00:00:54,725 --> 00:00:59,062
She was sent to her paternal
grandfather's village school in Kudat,

15
00:00:59,142 --> 00:01:01,962
then to Saint Monica's,
an Anglican boarding

16
00:01:02,042 --> 00:01:05,851
school in Sandakan, where a
teacher who could not pronounce

17
00:01:05,931 --> 00:01:09,425
her Chinese name gave her
the English name Elizabeth.

18
00:01:09,425 --> 00:01:13,360
In December 1929 she came
to Singapore to study at

19
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:16,332
the Convent of the Holy Infant Jesus,

20
00:01:16,412 --> 00:01:21,150
lodging with an uncle who ran
a music shop on Selegie Road.

21
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:26,369
Her mother died in 1931, and with the
Great Depression squeezing the family,

22
00:01:26,449 --> 00:01:29,716
Choy gave up a shot at a
college scholarship and

23
00:01:29,796 --> 00:01:34,050
went to work instead, to help
raise her six younger siblings.

24
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:38,259
She began teaching at the
Church of England Zenana Mission

25
00:01:38,339 --> 00:01:41,882
School (today's Saint
Margaret's School) in 1933,

26
00:01:41,962 --> 00:01:46,984
then transferred to Saint Andrew's
School in 1935, by her own account

27
00:01:47,064 --> 00:01:51,575
probably the only untrained
teacher on the staff at the time.

28
00:01:51,575 --> 00:01:55,659
On the 16th of August 1941
she married Choy Khun Heng,

29
00:01:55,739 --> 00:01:58,358
a bookkeeper at the Borneo Company,

30
00:01:58,438 --> 00:02:02,525
in a double wedding held
together with her brother's.

31
00:02:02,525 --> 00:02:07,150
Singapore fell to Japan in February 1942.

32
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:12,267
Choy and her husband found work running
a canteen at the Mental Hospital,

33
00:02:12,347 --> 00:02:15,471
renamed Miyako Hospital
under the occupation,

34
00:02:15,551 --> 00:02:20,250
which had taken in patients
transferred from the General Hospital.

35
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:24,589
It gave them a reason to move
around the city, and they used it:

36
00:02:24,669 --> 00:02:27,972
the Choys secretly carried
food, medicine, money,

37
00:02:28,052 --> 00:02:32,322
letters and, at real risk to
themselves, radio parts to British

38
00:02:32,402 --> 00:02:36,200
civilian internees and
prisoners of war held at Changi.

39
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:41,399
The end of their smuggling operation
traces back to Operation Jaywick,

40
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:44,944
a September 1943 raid in
which Allied commandos

41
00:02:45,024 --> 00:02:49,775
slipped into Singapore Harbour
and sank several Japanese ships.

42
00:02:49,775 --> 00:02:55,553
The Kempeitai, unable to believe the raid
had been mounted from outside Singapore,

43
00:02:55,633 --> 00:02:58,982
concluded it was the work
of local sympathisers,

44
00:02:59,062 --> 00:03:04,269
and on the 10th of October 1943 —
"Double Tenth" — began a wave of arrests

45
00:03:04,349 --> 00:03:08,850
and interrogations across the
civilian and internee population.

46
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:12,251
Fifty-seven people were
eventually detained;

47
00:03:12,331 --> 00:03:16,050
fifteen died under
interrogation or in custody.

48
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:19,176
An informant told the Kempeitai the Choys

49
00:03:19,256 --> 00:03:22,150
had been smuggling money into Changi.

50
00:03:22,150 --> 00:03:26,625
Her husband was arrested
on the 29th of October.

51
00:03:26,625 --> 00:03:30,814
Choy went to the Kempeitai's
headquarters to ask after him;

52
00:03:30,894 --> 00:03:35,084
she was told they knew nothing,
then lured back three weeks

53
00:03:35,164 --> 00:03:39,650
later and held herself, arrested
on the 15th of November 1943.

54
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:43,894
Choy was interrogated at the
Kempeitai's East District Branch,

55
00:03:43,974 --> 00:03:48,916
in the old YMCA building on the site
described above, and never admitted

56
00:03:48,996 --> 00:03:53,600
to being a British sympathiser or
named anyone who had helped her.

57
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:58,800
She was starved and repeatedly
tortured over roughly 200 days.

58
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,608
R. H. Scott, a wartime director
at the British Ministry

59
00:04:02,688 --> 00:04:06,636
of Information who later
testified as a principal witness

60
00:04:06,716 --> 00:04:10,453
at Singapore's war crimes
trials, said he had seen her

61
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:14,350
stripped and severely beaten
on at least one occasion.

62
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:17,775
She credited her Christian
faith, and the moral texts

63
00:04:17,855 --> 00:04:21,625
she had learned as a schoolgirl,
with keeping her intact.

64
00:04:21,625 --> 00:04:26,450
Her husband was released some time
after her; both survived the war.

65
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:29,538
At the Japanese surrender
in September 1945,

66
00:04:29,618 --> 00:04:35,729
Lady Mountbatten personally invited Choy to
witness the ceremony, and she was escorted

67
00:04:35,809 --> 00:04:39,833
to it by the governor, Sir
Shenton Thomas, and his wife —

68
00:04:39,913 --> 00:04:45,025
the same Lady Thomas to whom Choy
had once sent medicine inside Changi.

69
00:04:45,025 --> 00:04:48,545
The Choys were invited
to England to recuperate,

70
00:04:48,625 --> 00:04:50,275
and stayed four years.

71
00:04:50,275 --> 00:04:54,273
Lady Baden-Powell awarded her
the Girl Guides' Bronze Cross,

72
00:04:54,353 --> 00:04:57,468
its highest honour for
gallantry, and the Rajah

73
00:04:57,548 --> 00:05:01,150
of Sarawak gave her the
Order of the Star of Sarawak.

74
00:05:01,150 --> 00:05:05,413
In June 1946 she and her husband
were made Officers of the Order

75
00:05:05,493 --> 00:05:09,484
of the British Empire, and on
the 25th of July that year she

76
00:05:09,564 --> 00:05:13,691
was given a private audience
with Queen Elizabeth, the wife of

77
00:05:13,771 --> 00:05:17,695
King George the Sixth, at Saint
James's Palace — a full six

78
00:05:17,775 --> 00:05:22,050
years before that Queen's daughter
became Elizabeth the Second.

79
00:05:22,050 --> 00:05:25,651
Choy studied domestic science
at Northern Polytechnic,

80
00:05:25,731 --> 00:05:31,513
taught briefly at a London council school,
and — unable to afford art school herself —

81
00:05:31,593 --> 00:05:35,467
worked as an artist's model
for the sculptor Dora Gordine,

82
00:05:35,547 --> 00:05:40,250
who cast her twice, in works titled
Serene Jade and Flawless Crystal.

83
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:45,000
Choy came back to
Singapore in December 1949.

84
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,330
The following year she
stood for the West Ward

85
00:05:48,410 --> 00:05:52,110
seat in the December 1950
City Council election and

86
00:05:52,190 --> 00:05:55,600
lost to the Progressive
Party's Soh Ghee Soon.

87
00:05:55,600 --> 00:06:00,228
The Governor nominated her to
the Legislative Council anyway,

88
00:06:00,308 --> 00:06:05,090
where she served a full term from
1951 to 1955 — the only woman

89
00:06:05,170 --> 00:06:09,800
on it — speaking often for the
poor and for social services.

90
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:13,694
As a Council member she
represented Singapore at the

91
00:06:13,774 --> 00:06:17,820
coronation of Queen Elizabeth
the Second in June 1953,

92
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:21,717
a second, distinct royal
occasion seven years after

93
00:06:21,797 --> 00:06:26,000
her private audience with
the previous Queen Elizabeth.

94
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,468
She stood for election once more,
in Queenstown, then left politics,

95
00:06:30,548 --> 00:06:33,625
believing she could do
more good as a teacher.

96
00:06:33,625 --> 00:06:37,723
She kept teaching at
Saint Andrew's until 1974,

97
00:06:37,803 --> 00:06:42,078
apart from a stint from
1956 to 1960 as the first

98
00:06:42,158 --> 00:06:46,425
principal of the Singapore
School for the Blind.

99
00:06:46,425 --> 00:06:50,558
She was known around the school
gates for her qipaos and bangles,

100
00:06:50,638 --> 00:06:54,966
which earned her the nickname "the
Dayak Woman of Singapore," and by

101
00:06:55,046 --> 00:06:59,373
the 1950s she had also joined the
women's auxiliary of the Singapore

102
00:06:59,453 --> 00:07:04,624
Volunteer Corps — where, per a 1953
newspaper headline announcing her enlistment,

103
00:07:04,704 --> 00:07:07,750
she picked up a second
nickname, "Gunner Choy."

104
00:07:07,750 --> 00:07:14,238
Elizabeth Choy died of pancreatic
cancer on the 14th of September 2006,

105
00:07:14,318 --> 00:07:17,925
at her home on MacKenzie Road, aged 95.

106
00:07:17,925 --> 00:07:21,994
Why it matters today: the
building where Elizabeth Choy was

107
00:07:22,074 --> 00:07:27,550
tortured for nearly 200 days without
giving up a single name is still standing,

108
00:07:27,630 --> 00:07:30,715
gazetted since 1995,
directly across the road

109
00:07:30,795 --> 00:07:35,216
from one of Singapore's most-visited
museums — and almost nobody

110
00:07:35,296 --> 00:07:39,375
waiting at the bus stop outside
it has any reason to know.

111
00:07:39,375 --> 00:07:44,396
Her name survives in a handful of
institutions and a Straits Times obituary,

112
00:07:44,476 --> 00:07:47,349
not in a street sign,
which makes the marker

113
00:07:47,429 --> 00:07:51,644
on that corner one of the few
physical things left that connects

114
00:07:51,724 --> 00:07:55,550
an ordinary building to what
was actually done inside it.
